Chapter: Cordic

 CORDIC (COordinate Rotation DIgital Computer) iterative algorithm
 for computing trig functions and sqrt using only shifts and adds.
 Perfect for bare-metal integer hardware. Fixed-point scale 1000.

 ACCURACY, MEASURED RATHER THAN CLAIMED. Worst absolute error over a full turn
 is 6 in 1000 -- 0.6 per cent of full scale. The SAME measurement read as a
 relative error is 3.7 per cent, because near a zero crossing a small absolute
 error is a large fraction of a small value, and near a peak the reverse. So an
 accuracy figure for this chapter means nothing without saying which of the two
 it is. This line said "16 iterations gives ~0.1% accuracy" and named neither.
 codex/test/forewords/math-cordic-accuracy pins the absolute one against real
 sine and cosine.

 SIXTEEN ITERATIONS IS TEN. The angle table's last six entries are zero: at
 scale 1000, atan(2^-k) in milliradians rounds to zero from k = 10 on, and the
 shift x / (1 << k) on a coordinate near 1000 truncates to zero alongside it.
 Those six iterations retire no angle and move neither coordinate. What bounds
 the result is the fixed-point scale, not the iteration count -- raising
 cordic-iterations alone would buy nothing.

 We say:

Section: Constants
  cordic-scale : Integer = 1000
  cordic-iterations : Integer = 16
  cordic-gain : Integer = 607
  cordic-half-pi : Integer = 1571
  cordic-pi : Integer = 3142
  cordic-three-half-pi : Integer = 4712
  cordic-two-pi : Integer = 6283

Section: Angle Table
  cordic-atan-table : List Integer
  cordic-atan-table = [785, 463, 244, 124, 62, 31, 15, 7, 3, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]

Section: Rotation Mode (cos/sin)
  CordicResult = record {
    cos-val : Integer,
    sin-val : Integer
  }

 The angle table above sums to 1735 milliradians, and the rotation cannot
 retire a target larger than that, so nothing outside [0, 1735] converges.

  cordic-sincos : Integer -> CordicResult
  cordic-sincos (angle-milli) =
    let a = cordic-normalize angle-milli
    in if a <= cordic-half-pi then cordic-rotate a cordic-scale 0 0
    else if a <= cordic-pi then let q = cordic-rotate (cordic-pi - a) cordic-scale 0 0
    in CordicResult { cos-val = 0 - q.cos-val, sin-val = q.sin-val }
    else if a <= cordic-three-half-pi then let q = cordic-rotate (a - cordic-pi) cordic-scale 0 0
    in CordicResult { cos-val = 0 - q.cos-val, sin-val = 0 - q.sin-val }
    else let q = cordic-rotate (cordic-two-pi - a) cordic-scale 0 0
    in CordicResult { cos-val = q.cos-val, sin-val = 0 - q.sin-val }

  cordic-normalize : Integer -> Integer
  cordic-normalize (a) =
    let full = 6283
    in let a2 = a - (a / full) * full
    in if a2 < 0 then a2 + full else a2

  cordic-rotate : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> CordicResult
  cordic-rotate (target) (x) (y) (i) =
    if i >= cordic-iterations then CordicResult { cos-val = x * cordic-gain / cordic-scale, sin-val = y * cordic-gain / cordic-scale }
    else let atan-i = list-at cordic-atan-table i
    in if target > 0 then cordic-rotate (target - atan-i) (x - (y / (bit-shl 1 i))) (y + (x / (bit-shl 1 i))) (i + 1)
    else cordic-rotate (target + atan-i) (x + (y / (bit-shl 1 i))) (y - (x / (bit-shl 1 i))) (i + 1)

Section: Individual Functions
  cordic-cos : Integer -> Integer
  cordic-cos (angle) = (cordic-sincos angle).cos-val

  cordic-sin : Integer -> Integer
  cordic-sin (angle) = (cordic-sincos angle).sin-val

  cordic-tan : Integer -> Integer
  cordic-tan (angle) =
    let sc = cordic-sincos angle
    in if sc.cos-val == 0 then 999999
    else sc.sin-val * cordic-scale / sc.cos-val

Section: Vectoring Mode (atan2, magnitude)
  CordicVector = record {
    angle : Integer,
    magnitude : Integer
  }

  cordic-atan2 : Integer, Integer -> CordicVector
  cordic-atan2 (y) (x) =
    let ax = if x < 0 then -x else x
    in let ay = if y < 0 then -y else y
    in let raw = cordic-vector ax ay 0 0
    in let mag = raw.magnitude * cordic-gain / cordic-scale
    in let angle = cordic-adjust-quadrant (raw.angle) x y
    in CordicVector { angle = angle, magnitude = mag }

  cordic-vector : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> CordicVector
  cordic-vector (x) (y) (angle) (i) =
    if i >= cordic-iterations then CordicVector { angle = angle, magnitude = x }
    else let atan-i = list-at cordic-atan-table i
    in if y > 0 then cordic-vector (x + (y / (bit-shl 1 i))) (y - (x / (bit-shl 1 i))) (angle + atan-i) (i + 1)
    else cordic-vector (x - (y / (bit-shl 1 i))) (y + (x / (bit-shl 1 i))) (angle - atan-i) (i + 1)

  cordic-adjust-quadrant : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cordic-adjust-quadrant (angle) (x) (y) =
    if x >= 0 then if y >= 0 then angle else -angle
    else if y >= 0 then 3141 - angle else angle - 3141

Section: Square Root
  cordic-sqrt : Integer -> Integer
  cordic-sqrt (n) =
    if n <= 0 then 0
    else cordic-sqrt-loop n (n / 2 + 1)

  cordic-sqrt-loop : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cordic-sqrt-loop (n) (guess) =
    let next = (guess + n / guess) / 2
    in if next >= guess then guess
    else cordic-sqrt-loop n next

